      <section class="interview-exchange" aria-labelledby="origins">
        <div class="interview-question"><span class="interview-speaker">Tequia Burt <span aria-hidden="true">01</span></span>
          <h2 id="origins" data-source-anchor="origins">How did you arrive at the concept of early epistemic settlement? Was there a particular writing experience or limitation in the existing research that led you to it?</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="interview-answer"><span class="interview-speaker">Henry Wang</span>
          <p>I started from a fairly simple observation about what AI changes in writing.</p>
          <p>Writing has often been treated as part of thinking, not just a way of recording thought after the thinking is finished. Menary&#x27;s Writing as Thinking captures this well (Menary, 2007). You write something, see what follows from it, realize that two claims do not quite fit, go back to the material, move things around, and sometimes end up thinking something you could not have stated when you began. The text develops along with your understanding.</p>
          <p>AI makes it much easier for those two things to come apart.</p>
          <p>Suppose I have two pieces of evidence and cannot yet work out how they fit into one argument. Previously, if I wanted the paragraph to work, I would usually have to form enough of that connection myself to write it. A teacher, editor, book, or colleague could of course help. But with a language model I can keep giving it the problem in front of me and get a coherent next step back.</p>
          <p>So the text can keep developing even when I did not have to build all of the connections that made that development possible.</p>
          <p>That made me wonder whether anything had been lost. If the argument is good, I understand it, and I can keep reasoning from it, is that enough?</p>
          <p>There was already work showing that AI changes what people produce. One result I found interesting was that AI assistance could improve individual creative outputs while also making different people&#x27;s outputs more similar (Doshi &amp; Hauser, 2024).</p>
          <p>What I wanted to understand was what was happening inside one person&#x27;s inquiry. What changed between the point where they were still trying to form a relation and the point where they had been given one that worked?</p>
          <p>My background in cognitive modelling probably also shaped this. I tend to think about how someone&#x27;s internal structure changes over time, so the finished text alone did not really explain what interested me. I wanted to know what happened to the writer&#x27;s own construction process and cognitive development through working with AI.</p>
          <p>That is where early epistemic settlement came from.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="interview-exchange" aria-labelledby="professional-writing">
        <div class="interview-question"><span class="interview-speaker">Tequia Burt <span aria-hidden="true">02</span></span>
          <h2 id="professional-writing" data-source-anchor="professional-writing">In professional writing, how might an AI response shape the direction of an inquiry even when the writer understands the response and continues thinking productively? Could you walk me through an example of the questions, connections, or alternative frames that might become less likely to emerge?</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="interview-answer"><span class="interview-speaker">Henry Wang</span>
          <p>Imagine a journalist has two interviews about hybrid work.</p>
          <p>One person says they are more productive at home because they can concentrate. Another says their team became less productive because coordination became harder.</p>
          <p>The journalist has not yet worked out what to do with those two observations.</p>
          <p>AI gives her a clean frame: hybrid work involves a trade-off between individual focus and team collaboration.</p>
          <p>That is a perfectly reasonable interpretation. She understands it and could write a good article from it.</p>
          <p>But if she had stayed with the mismatch for longer, she might have noticed that the first person&#x27;s work is mostly individual analysis, while the second person&#x27;s work depends heavily on coordination. The question could then change from “Is hybrid work good or bad for productivity?” to “Which kinds of work benefit from remote work, and which depend on physical coordination?”</p>
          <p>Now she may interview different people, look for different evidence, and end up writing a different article.</p>
          <p>The first frame was not wrong. It just resolved the difficulty before it had a chance to surface any further questions or connections.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="interview-exchange" aria-labelledby="timing-and-expertise">
        <div class="interview-question"><span class="interview-speaker">Tequia Burt <span aria-hidden="true">03</span></span>
          <h2 id="timing-and-expertise" data-source-anchor="timing-and-expertise">What determines whether an AI suggestion settles an inquiry too early or becomes useful material the writer actively works through? How much depends on timing, the writer’s expertise, or the nature of the task?</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="interview-answer"><span class="interview-speaker">Henry Wang</span>
          <p>I think this depends on what the writer is doing at the moment the suggestion arrives.</p>
          <p>Timing matters. If I already understand a relation and just cannot phrase it well, AI may be helping me say something I already know. If trying to write the sentence is how I am still figuring out the relation, the same answer can do something quite different.</p>
          <p>Expertise probably matters too. An expert usually has more existing relations and constraints for a new suggestion to fit with. Something that sounds coherent to a novice may immediately feel wrong to an expert because it clashes with something else they know.</p>
          <p>The nature of the task matters as well. Some tasks have a fairly constrained destination but still require substantial construction from the person doing them. If the goal is only to produce a correct artifact, skipping some of that construction may not matter much. If the construction itself is part of how the person is learning, then the same shortcut changes what they get from doing the task.</p>
          <p>But this distinction depends on being able to tell how constrained the task really is. From inside an inquiry, that is often difficult to know. You may be slowly working toward something fairly standard, or you may be one connection away from seeing the material differently. You have to decide whether to keep working before you know what that work would produce.
That uncertainty seems important to me. It makes the value of continuing difficult to judge from the writer&#x27;s own position.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="interview-exchange" aria-labelledby="models-and-collaborators">
        <div class="interview-question"><span class="interview-speaker">Tequia Burt <span aria-hidden="true">04</span></span>
          <h2 id="models-and-collaborators" data-source-anchor="models-and-collaborators">Editors and other collaborators can also provide a solution before a writer has completed the underlying thinking. What makes language models different, beyond the speed and frequency with which they can intervene?</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="interview-answer"><span class="interview-speaker">Henry Wang</span>
          <p>What seems different to me is how much independent structure tends to enter the exchange.</p>
          <p>When another person works with my material, they are processing it through their own understanding. In the hybrid-work example, an editor might notice that one interviewee&#x27;s work depends much more heavily on coordination than the other&#x27;s. She might also wonder whether the difference has less to do with remote work itself than with how the two teams are managed. Those relations come partly from what she already knows and from the connections she makes while thinking through the material.</p>
          <p>I may not have needed either of those relations just to move forward. But once she brings them back, I have more to think with, and they may change where the inquiry goes.</p>
          <p>A very common way of using a language model is different. I bring it whatever problem is blocking me at the moment and ask how I might continue. If it finds a locally coherent connection that gets me past that difficulty, the interaction has already done what I needed it to do.</p>
          <p>There may be many other relevant relations the model could surface, but the immediate problem does not require them. It can give me enough structure to continue without expanding the space very much beyond the question I was already able to ask.</p>
          <p>That can then repeat throughout the draft. I reach a difficulty, get a satisfactory way through it, continue from there, and later return with the next difficulty. Each answer can be useful on its own while the inquiry develops through a sequence of these local resolutions.</p>
          <p>That is the pattern I was trying to capture in the paper. A collaborator brings a separately developed structure into the exchange almost by default simply because they have one. With a language model, it is very easy to keep getting help that is closely fitted to the problem as I have already formulated it.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="interview-exchange" aria-labelledby="studying-inquiry">
        <div class="interview-question"><span class="interview-speaker">Tequia Burt <span aria-hidden="true">05</span></span>
          <h2 id="studying-inquiry" data-source-anchor="studying-inquiry">What would an empirical study need to observe or capture to test this framework? How might researchers distinguish an avenue of inquiry that was foreclosed from one the writer simply considered and rejected?</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="interview-answer"><span class="interview-speaker">Henry Wang</span>
          <p>I&#x27;m still working this one out.</p>
          <p>I think there are two different questions hiding inside it. One is whether early settlement happened. The other is whether it was a bad trade.</p>
          <p>The first seems more straightforward to study.</p>
          <p>You could follow what a writer was doing immediately before and after assistance arrived. What were they trying to work out? Which notes or sources were they using? What was still unresolved? After they accept the response, does that attempt continue, or do the next questions start from the structure the response supplied?</p>
          <p>You could also manipulate when the help arrives. One group gets it immediately, another after some independent work. Or vary what kind of help they receive.</p>
          <p>I would be interested in what happens later, not only whether the final text is better. What questions do people ask next? What distinctions appear later in the argument? Does changing when or how assistance arrives change the kinds of relations that get developed?</p>
          <p>For the distinction you asked about between a foreclosed avenue and one the writer considered and rejected, I think the important question is whether the alternative ever became developed enough to be evaluated. A rejected idea should leave some trace. It may appear in notes, a draft, a question the writer asked, or an explanation of why they decided against it. With foreclosure, there may only be an unresolved tension or a partially forming connection before the assistance arrives, and then that line of work disappears.</p>
          <p>We still could not say that one particular missing idea definitely would have appeared. But at least we could study whether different kinds of assistance systematically change which relations get far enough to be considered.</p>
          <p>The harder question is whether whatever was lost was worth more than the time and effort AI saved.</p>
          <p>That depends a lot on what the person is trying to do. If I need a workable recommendation this afternoon, stopping once I have a good answer may be entirely sensible. If I am doing research and the point is to find a better explanation, I may be willing to spend much longer following uncertain directions.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="interview-exchange" aria-labelledby="designing-assistance">
        <div class="interview-question"><span class="interview-speaker">Tequia Burt <span aria-hidden="true">06</span></span>
          <h2 id="designing-assistance" data-source-anchor="designing-assistance">Are there practices that could help writers use AI without bypassing the work that expands what they are able to question, defend, or develop?</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="interview-answer"><span class="interview-speaker">Henry Wang</span>
          <p>I think there are two separate parts of the interaction that matter here.</p>
          <p>One is what the AI contributes. If I am stuck between two ideas, it can give me the connection needed to move forward. But it could also surface other relations around the problem that are not necessary for solving the immediate difficulty and might open up other directions.</p>
          <p>The other is how that contribution is presented.</p>
          <p>The same underlying connection could arrive as a finished interpretation, a question, a tension to resolve, two competing frames, or a counterexample. Those forms leave very different amounts of construction for the writer to do.</p>
          <p>There is some evidence that this difference in form matters. In Don&#x27;t Just Tell Me, Ask Me, framing similar information as questions rather than direct explanations led people to reason with it differently (Danry et al., 2023). That is close to what I have in mind here. The contribution can be similar, while the way it is presented changes what the person still has to work out for themselves.</p>
          <p>Sometimes a finished answer is exactly what I want. If I have already done the conceptual work and am stuck on wording, there may be little value in making the interaction more elaborate.</p>
          <p>The more difficult case is when I am still trying to understand the material. Then I would want the system to help without simply turning every local difficulty into a stopping point. That could mean leaving some of the construction for me to do, or bringing in connections that expand the problem beyond the one I have already formulated.</p>
          <p>I still don&#x27;t know exactly how a system should decide what to contribute, or in what form, given the task and the writer&#x27;s state at that moment. That&#x27;s something I&#x27;m interested in pursuing next.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
